I wrote the session plan: two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit didn't happen because session_plan/ is gitignored. The files are on disk, and the instruction's || true already allows for the commit failing this way.

Task 1 (evolve, `scripts/extract_trajectory.py` only): this session's trajectory said "these are live" about CI failures, but CI has been green for a week. The log line from that check shows why. gh returned a list of runs from a week ago, and its newest run was the failure itself. The function that spots an out-of-date list (page_is_stale) counts that tie as up to date, so the old failure read as still live. The fix doesn't turn a tie into "could not check", because a tie is also exactly what a real live failure looks like. Instead, on a tie it runs a second, unfiltered run listing to settle it:
• If that listing shows a newer run, the first list was out of date and the failure is no longer reported as live.
• If it agrees, the failure is reported as live, the same as today.
• If it fails, the result is "could not check".

The test cases use the real values from today's log line. One case checks that a real live failure is still reported word for word as before. The task also includes deliberately breaking the check once to confirm a test catches it.

Task 2 (product, part of #988): after Ctrl-C while a tool is running, does the next turn send a tool_use with no matching tool_result? If it does, every later turn in that REPL session would fail with an API 400. The task measures this first with a stub provider and records the raw messages.
• If the gap is real: add a tool_result marked as an error and cancelled for each unpaired call, and pin it with a test.
• If it isn't: keep the probe as a regression test and record the result only as "no unpaired tool_use after a mid-tool cancel".

It stays out of main.rs, because the trajectory warned that main took 4 of the last 7 changes.

